  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 312 AM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nMain forecast highlights for Monday are wind and precipitation \npotential. \n\nWe are forecast to be in a weak, mostly southwest flow aloft with a \nweak jet streak overhead. Previous model runs showed a much stronger \njet streak over our area. There is also a weak trough just west of \nthe Rocky Mountains with a second deeper trough digging over the \nPacific. A strong cold front is forecast to move in from the \nnorthwest setting the stage for a potential high wind day for the \ncounty warning area (CWA). A surface low will set up sometime Monday \nafternoon, but modes diverge on its location, creating uncertainty \nin the forecast. ECMWF ensembles have the low setting up somewhere \nin Nebraska while GFS ensembles forecast the low in northwest Kansas \nor southwest Nebraska. Canadian ensembles are split roughly 50/50 \nbetween the ECMWF and GFS solutions. Another notable difference in \nensembles is the GFS is showing a much deeper surface low, which is \nsupportive of a tighter pressure gradient and stronger wind \npotential. So while there is uncertainty in the low location, we \nhave all the necessary ingredients for a high wind day, the \nintensity is the main question. Where the low sets up and how strong \nit becomes will determine the severity of the winds. Sky cover will \nbe another factor. Clear skies will allow stronger synoptic \nwinds to mix down to the surface, while cloudy skies will serve \nas a barrier for stronger winds to make it to the surface.\n\nWinds are expected to pick up in the afternoon after the cold front \nfrom the northwest moves through. The entire CWA should expect to \nsee windy conditions, but the counties along and west of the \nColorado/Kansas/Nebraska borders will see the strongest winds with \ngusts up to 60 mph possible. The eastern CWA could see gusts up to \n40 mph. \n\nPrecipitation wise, showers and storms are expected to fire off in \nthe afternoon following the morning cold frontal passage. Southwest \nflow aloft will send several embedded shortwaves and associated \nvorticity maxima across the CWA Monday afternoon. Probability of \nPrecipitation (PoPs) are around 50-75%, increasing from west to \neast. The western CWA will likely receive just rain, while the \neastern CWA has enough instability to support a weak thunderstorm or \ntwo. \n\nBlowing dust will be something to keep an eye out for Monday. Lapse \nrates are not supportive of dust being lofted, and the recent \nstretch of dense fog will help keep the ground saturated. \nPrecipitation will also help limit the potential for blowing dust, \nalthough PoPs for eastern Colorado are only around 50%. Areas that \nmiss out on Monday's precipitation will be a concern for blowing \ndust if they can dry out enough.\n\nTuesday is forecast to be another windy day as another jet streak \nscrapes the CWA. While not as strong, wind gusts up to 50 mph are \nstill possible for the western CWA. This will really depend on what \nthe upper-level flow does Monday and where the jet sets up. Models \ndiverge on the pattern aloft after Monday, so the jet streak could \nshift south of our area, diminishing the wind threat. Blowing\ndust is also on the table, but how Monday pans out will \ndetermine the threat.\n\nTemperatures for the long term period are fairly consistent. \nMonday's highs are forecast in the 50s to 60s with potential for the \nfar eastern CWA to see some 70s. Tuesday through Friday, expect high \ntemperatures in the 50s with lows in the 30s to 40s. Our eastern \nColorado counties could see lows around freezing through Friday.",
